This study aims to fill this knowledge gap by exploring the relationship between noise pollution and tree coverage in Los Angeles, California, a city plagued with excessive noise levels exceeding 65 decibels during the day. By analyzing the connection between noise levels and green space, this research aims to provide valuable insights for policymakers and city planners in mitigating noise exposure for citizens. With a focus on developing an open dataset for verification purposes, this study utilizes noise data collected from the LACoGS project, a collaborative effort of the City of Los Angeles' Office of Sustainability and the University of Southern California. With a spatial resolution of 100 meters and a temporal resolution of 15 minutes, this dataset enables detailed analysis of noise levels in the Los Angeles area. A machine learning model incorporating random forest regression, which can handle complex, high-dimensional data, was trained on this dataset. The model's performance was further enhanced through the use of Geographical Weighted Regression, which accounts for the varying noise patterns in the area. By training the model with the LACoGS dataset, this research demonstrates its ability to accurately predict noise levels, with a high coefficient of determination, and can be used to provide a spatial resolution of 10 meters. This study's methodology can be applied to other noise prediction tasks, making it a valuable resource for researchers in the field.

